## Code lookups: which tool to use

Pick the cheapest tool that answers the question. Ask for names only first, then fetch details
for the few items you need. Never read a whole AOT XML file to answer a simple question.

| Question | Tool | Typical cost |
|---|---|---|
| Who reads, writes or calls field or method X (standard code) | `xref.ps1 uses Owner.member` | < 500 tokens, < 1 s |
| CoC extensions, table/form extensions, event handlers of X | `d365fo find coc / extensions / handlers` | 0.5k–3k tokens, ~1 s |
| Tables with a field of EDT X, relations, field list | `xref.ps1 fields-by-edt / relations / fields` | 0.1k–2k tokens |
| Full structure of one object | `d365fo get table/class/form X` | up to 44k tokens for a large table |
| Exact text or a pattern | `grep -l` on the model folders only | small output, 20–50 s on all packages |
| Create or change code | `d365fo modify / generate` | — |
| Check code before a build | `d365fo validate / bp`, `d365fo test` | — |

Avoid: `d365fo find refs --xref` (can take 10+ minutes), `-o table` output (40–90% larger than JSON),
reading AOT XML files over 100 KB.

### Freshness

- The cross-reference database only knows code from the last build with "Update cross references".
  Check with `xref.ps1 status`.
- The CLI index only knows code from the last `d365fo index refresh` (or `index sync <model>`).
  Check with `d365fo index status`.
- For code changed after that, refresh the index first or use `grep`, which reads the files directly.

### Traps

- The CLI names models, not packages: ApplicationSuite's model is `Foundation`.
  A wrong `--model` returns 0 results without an error.
- Cross-reference paths: code references look like `/Tables/SalesLine/Fields/SalesPrice`;
  metadata looks like `Table/SalesLine/TableFieldReal/SalesPrice`. `[ExtensionOf]` is stored as `/Classes/ExtensionOf`.
- Field references don't tell reads from writes. "Who writes X" needs a look at the code.
- Treat 0 results as suspicious: cross-check with a second tool before concluding there are none.

### Session type

- Lookup-only session: use the cross-reference database and grep; load the CLI only for extension questions.
- Development session: load the CLI; it is the only tool that writes, validates and tests.
